    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d357">

                <pb facs="0374" n="1052" sameAs="frus:frus1863p2/pg1052"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Pruyn</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 19.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1862-09-25"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >September</hi> 25, 1862.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your very interesting despatch of May 26
                    (No. 22) has been received. It is to be hoped that the change in the department
                    of foreign affairs which has been made by his Majesty will be followed by no
                    departure from the enlightened policy towards the Western Powers which was
                    pursued while Ando Tsusima No Kami was connected with that department.</p>
                <p>The President learns with sincere pleasure that your relations with the
                    government are cordial and satisfactory.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert H. Pruyn</hi>, Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., Yedo.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
